## Guiding Principles
- **Low friction:** Minimize cursor travel and eliminate redundant clicks.
- **Clear feedback:** Actions trigger visible and audible confirmation; disabled states explain themselves.
- **Focus retention:** Keep the player’s attention on the garden, not on UI chrome.
- **Continuous progress:** Every interaction should feel productive, even when blocked (explain why).
- **Cursor-led communication:** The cursor icon and state must always represent the highest-priority action available under the pointer.

---

## Game Flow Overview
- Players manage a loop of planting, growing, harvesting, and expanding soil tiles using fruit.
- Planting and Building operate in explicit modes; harvesting remains omnipresent and overrides other intents.
- Future systems (automation, upgrades) must respect this loop while reducing friction further.

---

## Cursor Priorities

| Priority | Condition | Cursor Icon / Action |
|----------|-----------|----------------------|
| 1 | Tile contains mature plant | Harvest cursor → harvest on click |
| 2 | Build Mode active + valid highlight | Build cursor → place soil |
| 3 | Build Mode active + invalid highlight | Disabled build cursor |
| 4 | Plant Mode active + valid soil | Plant cursor → plant seed |
| 5 | Plant Mode active + invalid target or no seeds | Disabled plant cursor |
| 6 | Water Mode active + valid plant | Water cursor → apply water |
| 7 | Water Mode active + invalid target or out of water | Disabled water cursor |

Cursor rules must never conflict with harvest interactions, drag states, or feedback animations. When the cursor is disabled, reinforce the reason via UI buttons/tooltips.

---

## Mode Reference

### Build Mode (`B`)
- Cursor displays a soil tile icon.
- Click highlighted tiles to place soil (costs fruit); unavailable placements show disabled cursor/button.
- Exit via right-click or `Esc`.

### Plant Mode (Seed Hotkeys)
- Cursor shows the selected seed icon (e.g., `1` for Lumen Bloom).
- Click empty soil to plant; repeat until seeds run out or mode is canceled.
- Invalid targets or missing seeds display disabled feedback.
- Exit via right-click or `Esc`.

### Water Mode (`W`)
- Cursor swaps to a watering-can icon and remains active until canceled or another mode is chosen.
- Drag watering is supported: hold to water while moving; release to stop.
- Out-of-water state greys the cursor and blocks watering.

---

## Harvesting
- Always enabled and overrides any active mode.
- Clicking a mature plant immediately harvests fruit and/or seeds.
- Trigger harvest visuals and audio every time (particles, animations, sound cues).

---

## Mode Switching Rules
- Selecting a seed activates its Plant Mode; selecting Build shuts down Plant Mode.
- Pressing `W` activates Water Mode; pressing it again or choosing another mode deactivates it.
- Canceling via right-click or `Esc` leaves the player with no active mode (all actions disabled and clearly communicated).
- “No active mode” is purely a disabled state communicated by cursor and button styling; it is not a gameplay condition.

---

## Water Mode Deep Dive

### Interaction Overview
- **Activate:** Watering Can button or `W`.
- **Hold / Drag:** Mouse down begins watering, draining the water bar, emitting droplet particles within active AOE.
- **Release:** Mouse up halts water usage and particle emission.
- **Deactivate:** Press `W` again or select another mode.

### Target Behaviors

| Target Type | Result |
|-------------|--------|
| Plant tile | Raises `waterLevel`; reaching 1.0 sets `isFullyWatered`, applies `growthMultiplier` boost, starts `growthBoostTimer`. |
| Fully watered plant | Plays minimal splash FX without consuming water. |
| Dry soil / empty tile | No effect; cursor shows disabled state. |
| Out of water | Greyed cursor; watering attempts do nothing. |

### State Logic
- Plants track `waterLevel` (0–1), `isFullyWatered`, `growthBoostTimer`, and `growthMultiplier`.
- Water level decays over time (baseline `-0.005/sec`); re-watering is needed until automation upgrades exist.
- Keep particle emission throttled to avoid overdraw during fast cursor movement.

### Implementation Notes
- Register `WaterMode` through the shared `cursorRules` system at parity with other modes.
- Ensure watering works alongside tile hover detection for planting/harvesting.
- Respect priority order (Harvest > Build > Plant > Water > Disabled) when resolving hover states.

---

## Feedback & UX Standards
- **Visual:** Every interaction (successful or blocked) yields visible feedback—particles, glow, icon shift.
- **Audio:** Distinct sound cues for planting, harvesting, building, and watering.
- **Disabled States:** Cursor and UI buttons mirror the disabled style and communicate the reason (no seeds, insufficient fruit, invalid tile, empty water bar).
- **Mode Persistence:** The active mode persists until canceled or replaced; never force extra clicks.

---

## Upgrade Hooks

| Upgrade | Effect on Water Mode |
|---------|---------------------|
| Reservoir Level | Expands water bar capacity (+20%/level baseline). |
| Water Speed | Lowers watering time per plant (≈ -10%/level). |
| Water AOE | Increases droplet radius. |
| Automation (future) | Enables periodic auto-watering pulses; not a cursor mode. |

Water Mode must pull values from the shared upgrade manager to keep UI and gameplay synchronized.

---

## Future-Proof Notes

| Feature | Integration Guidance |
|---------|----------------------|
| Sprinklers | Treat as automated watering events; do not create a new cursor mode. |
| Rain events | Temporarily disable Water Mode (greyed icon) and visually water all plants. |
| Fertilizer / soil moisture | Extend `plant.waterLevel` logic to interact with new resources without breaking existing behavior. |

---

## Example Player Flow
1. Press `1` to enter Plant Mode (Lumen Bloom). Cursor shows the seed icon.
2. Plant three soil tiles consecutively.
3. A plant ripens; clicking it harvests immediately even while Plant Mode is active.
4. Press `B` to switch to Build Mode and place soil on a highlighted tile.
5. Right-click to cancel Build Mode; cursor and buttons return to disabled state until a new mode is chosen.

---

## Design Principle Reminder
> Every player click must either perform an action or clearly communicate why it cannot.  
> Empty or confusing clicks are considered design failures.
